WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.136
-Our next guest is a talented
actress, comedienne and writer

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.071
you know her from her work
on "Catastrophe"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.806
and "The Unbearable Weight of
Massive Talent."

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:10.076
The season finale of her show
"Bad Sisters" is streaming

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.645
this Friday, October 14th,
on Apple TV+.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.046
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:16.783
-We can't just kill
our brother-in-law.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:19.185
Bibi, do I wish he was dead?

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:21.454
Yeah. He is a piece of [bleep].

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:25.725
But that is not how -- you know,
that's not how life --

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.027
society works. Okay?

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:30.764
You can't just explode a man.

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:32.665
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.667
our friend Sharon Horgan,
everybody.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.602
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:43.743 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:50.850 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.686
I am so happy to have you here
in person.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.589
-I'm so happy to be here.
I just met Pierce Brosnan.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:57.724
-Was it exciting for you?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.459
-Oh, my God, yeah.

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:02.161
Well, it was annoying at first
'cause he looks younger than me.

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:04.130
-Yeah.
-But he's from Drogheda.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.366
He's from the same place
in Ireland as me.

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:09.936
-That's a really -- That must be
a big deal, right?

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:12.571
-It is a big deal. I mean, it's
a bit of a one-horse town.

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:17.944
It's mainly famous for -- well,
it's got -- in its main church

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:21.781
in the city, it has the head of
a Saint, Oliver Plunkett,

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.516
who was hung, drawn,
and quartered.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.718
And you used to get taken
to see this head

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.386
when you were a youngster
on a little school trip.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:30.389
-Is it in a glass case?
-Of course. Yeah.

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:32.425
-Don't "of course."
We don't have our --

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.393
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:35.094
We don't leave our heads
out here.

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:39.365
-Well, we just love
a bit of gore, I guess.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:40.967
But yeah, it was lovely
to meet him.

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:44.904
He was like, "What the hell?"
But I was like, "I'm coming in."

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.505
-Is it hard? Because obviously,

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:48.975
you've been incredibly
successful.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.377
Is it hard that in your
hometown, they're like,

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.279
"Well, you're not quite
Pierce Brosnan, are you?"

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.514
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:58.651
-In my own family,
you know, I'm number two.

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.654
[ Laughter ]
My brother is an international,

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.357
or was an international
rugby-playing superstar.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:08.761
-Oh, really? You're not even
the number-one Horgan?

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.230
-I mean, I'm not even number
two, if I'm honest.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.398
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:14.900
My other brother is doing really
well for himself.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.003
-Do you think you could help us
book one of them?

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:19.438
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:21.941
-I would love that.
You'd really like them.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.510
-So, this is -- actually, since
we're talking about your family,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.912
this is a show about

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:28.514
five sisters
who are incredibly close.

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:30.550
I think the clip gives away to
some degree they're trying

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:35.388
to figure out what to do about
one of their brother-in-laws.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.023
But you know, it's about
a family that's very close.

00:02:38.023 --> 00:02:41.227
Does your family watch it? How
do your siblings feel about it?

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:44.797
-They love it, actually.
They hadn't seen any of it.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.867
So they're kind of watching it
as it goes out.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:50.970
So, I'm from a family of five.
So, I mean, that's part of

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.438
the reason why it was so much
fun to write,

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:55.741
just to put that, you know,
that joyous thing of just

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:58.411
being an instant tribe
when you have a big family.

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.580
But yeah, they love it.
They're really supportive.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:06.051
-This was a Belgian show
that you adapted.

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:08.421
How did you first
become aware of it?

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.089
-Well, I've been looking for
a Belgian show

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.825
to adapt for a long time.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.494
Finally.

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.196
-Every day, you call your agent,
you're like, "Anything?"

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.565
-Anything Flemish.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:24.336
So, two American writers,
Brett Baer and Dave Finkel,

00:03:24.336 --> 00:03:27.973
they had got the rights for it.

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:31.310
And I was looking for something
post-catastrophe,

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.813
sort of, you know, was tinkering
around with a few ideas.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:38.418
And the lady who's head of Apple
in the U.K., Jay Hunt,

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:40.353
she said, "I think I've got
something for you."

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:45.058
And I watched one episode
and I was in.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:47.994
I mean, it's such
a crazy premise.

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:53.098
These sisters, one of them is
married to a really odious,

00:03:53.098 --> 00:03:58.771
terrible, racist, sexist
misogynist, and also a prick,

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:01.440
you know.
Just a really awful man.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.775
-You listed the first one,

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:05.444
but also, putting his good
qualities aside.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:07.913
[ Laughter ]
-He's just a real prick.

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.516
-Yeah, he's a real prick.
Was it hard to write?

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:14.753
It's one of the more haunting
pricks I've seen on television.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:16.522
-Yeah. Well, thank you.
-Yeah, you're welcome.

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:20.292
-A lot of that is
down to Claes Bang.

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:22.562
-He's really spectacular.
-I love him.

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:26.298
He was our casting lady's idea.

00:04:26.298 --> 00:04:30.536
And he's Danish, really good
at playing pricks.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:33.806
[ Laughter ]
Actor. And yeah.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:36.609
But it was hard to write just
because we had to --

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.578
there's an abusive domestic
relationship at the heart of it.

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:44.817
And you want to -- that's a lot
of responsibility, you know,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:47.586
to tell a story like that and to
make it truthful and show

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:49.989
the, you know, brutality of it.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:53.226
But at the same time,
it's a comedy/drama.

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:56.095
And he's a clown and a fool.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:05:00.733
And so it was hard but fun
and a lot of responsibility.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:04.070
But you know.
I think we got it in the end.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.171
-Obviously, you're an actor
and you're a writer on it.

00:05:06.171 --> 00:05:09.242
You're also an E.P.,
and sometimes when E.P.s

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:13.245
have to make the kind of
decisions that other people

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:17.115
might not understand, the sort
of day-to-day choices you make,

00:05:17.115 --> 00:05:20.720
including -- Well, you explain
the photo real quick.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.987
And then we'll let you
read the caption.

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:23.422
What's going on there?

00:05:23.422 --> 00:05:26.058
-Okay. So, that --

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:28.928
that's actually from
the first day of filming.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:32.298
And it's the opening scene
of a show.

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:35.467
And that's Claes Bang there
in the coffin.

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:36.969
-He'll be really happy
this is the picture we're using.

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:41.540
-He will 'cause he looks great
in flannel.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:46.245
So, when you -- the opening
scene of a show, you have to --

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:48.981
you know, you're trying to bring
an audience in.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.516
And you want them to feel
confident

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.618
that they've chosen
the right show.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:56.055
So of course, I thought,
give a corpse a boner.

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:57.456
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.456 --> 00:06:01.661
-And then people will feel like,
"Okay, they've got this."

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:03.596
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:05.465
They know what they're doing.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:08.734
-And you wrote out
a lot of the, you know,

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:11.236
the sort of decisions
you were making.

00:06:11.236 --> 00:06:12.738
Too small and you can't see it.

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:14.774
Too large and it just upstages
the corpse.

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.843
-Yeah, also an issue --

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:19.078
slope needed to balance
the wedding photo.

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:20.446
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:22.848
It needed a sort of gradient.
-Yeah.

00:06:22.848 --> 00:06:25.818
-Because his widow panics when
she sees that he has,

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.087
technical term,
postmortem priapism.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.356
-Yeah.
-And so she has to cover it.

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:33.625
And she tries the flowers first
and then she uses their wedding

00:06:33.625 --> 00:06:37.629
photo, and then it just keeps
sort of just sliding off.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:38.864
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.864 --> 00:06:41.133
-Just -- It was --
-I will say, I was watching,

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:42.702
and I go, "That's a really
believable dead boner.

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:43.703
I'm going to stick with
this show."

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:45.638
-Thank you so much.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:47.072
-We've talked about
your daughters in the past.

00:06:47.072 --> 00:06:50.709
The last time we Zoom
interviewed, you were saying

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:54.813
how hard they are on you and how
honest they are with criticisms.

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:56.548
I believe one of them told you

00:06:56.548 --> 00:06:57.750
your eyes are too close
together.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.452
-Oh, yeah.
I mean, they are clearly.

00:06:59.452 --> 00:07:00.886
[ Laughter ]

00:07:00.886 --> 00:07:03.856
That was only when she was
really angry at me.

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:05.658
-Okay, gotcha.
-And I was shouting at her.

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:07.426
And probably my eyes did look
too close together.

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:09.562
-Well, I will say when you yell,
they do get a little --

00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:11.998
[ Laughter ]
-They do narrow in.

00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:14.767
No, they're great.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.336
I've got an 18-year-old
and a 14-year-old.

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:21.941
The 18-year-old is, you know,
I kind of rely on her a lot

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:24.744
to tell me what she thinks of
the shows

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:27.412
I'm working on at the time
because I feel like you need

00:07:27.412 --> 00:07:31.951
someone what can really sort of
break your creative confidence

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.687
at the beginning.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:38.357
When I show her stuff, I show
her really early in the edit

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.359
'cause her response is always,

00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:42.194
"It's not going to stay like
that, is it?"

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:45.164
[ Laughter ]
And then you're like, "No, no.

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:47.666
Why? What's wrong with it?"

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:50.302
And then, my 14-year-old
is too much of a legend

00:07:50.302 --> 00:07:52.004
to watch anything.
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:54.740
-If I ask her why she hasn't
watched it she goes,

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:00.479
"Well, you know, I haven't got
the log-in number for Apple TV"

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:01.914
or something like that.
There's always like --

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:03.348
-She used that excuse?
-Yeah.

00:08:03.348 --> 00:08:05.884
-It's never just, "I don't want
to watch it"?

00:08:05.884 --> 00:08:08.554
-Yeah. And then it's my fault
'cause they are convinced

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.022
that I don't know how to work
the house.

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:14.059
[ Laughter ]
Since I became a single parent,

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:17.162
they just think I don't know
how to make the house work.

00:08:17.162 --> 00:08:19.598
-So basically, you can write,
produce and star in

00:08:19.598 --> 00:08:21.600
an Apple show but you can't log
into Apple on the TV.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.868
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:25.904
I mean, that is true.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:25.904 --> 00:08:28.840
But then I'll just send them
a little review

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.209
that I got from something.
-That's nice.

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:31.510
-And it makes no difference.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.212
-Isn't it really funny how needy
we get with our kids?

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:36.448
-Oh, my God. I honest to God
sent them one

00:08:36.448 --> 00:08:38.450
like 10 minutes
before I came on.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:41.520
I sent this review, and I just
sent it with a little

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.323
sort of "uh" emoji, and they're
like, "Good one, Mum."

00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:47.059
[ Laughter ]
That's it.

00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:48.427
-Well, it is a good one.

00:08:48.427 --> 00:08:49.928
It is always lovely to see you.

00:08:49.928 --> 00:08:50.962
Thanks so much for being here.

00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:52.998
You guys, that's Sharon Horgan.

00:08:52.998 --> 00:08:55.534
The season finale of
"Bad Sisters" is streaming

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:57.002
this Friday on Apple TV+.

00:08:57.002 --> 00:08:58.237
We'll be right back with music

00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:00.339
from Craig Finn
and the Uptown Controllers.

